d be worth about 11 billion  the time was not worth 11 billion  dollars  second  is that from what we can tell  there has been no  court case  nobody has been proven to do this  there's no admittance of guilt or  anything  what we have is a journalist who is  pointing and saying you did it  they have sources they have some  evidence in terms of being able to use  some of the uh analytics tools chain  analysis etc  and so they believe that this is the  person who did it  but there is no  guarantee of it and so this to me is one  of those like slippery slopes right is  obviously we want journalists to be  doing the work in order to  help break open cases like this to do  journalism right et cetera at the same  time though is this guy let's say let's  give him the benefit of the doubt  presume that he did not do it he said he  didn't do it so let's just say for  example in a hypothetical situation that  he did not do it  imagine having your name drug through  the mainstream media  as being the hacker of 11 billion  dollars  and not having the 11 billion dollars  not having done it and having no way to  clear your name right in the sense of uh  uh in in the mainstream press  pretty crazy stuff and so i think that  this is one of those situations where  like we don't want to discourage  journalists from doing their job uh the  evidence again  uh from what i've read already this  morning seems to be uh fairly compelling  but at the same time there's this  balance between like people should be  innocent until proven guilty and when we  put their names out into the public  arena  it creates a scenario where i think  folks say  damn in the court of public opinion that  person's guilty maybe maybe not i don't  know but  will we ever actually get you know if he  if he didn't do it will we ever get that  known in public as much as it's known  that you know he's accused of doing it  yeah that that sucks right because if he  didn't do it one he doesn't have the  money so  and two his name was basically would  that be okay if he didn't have the money  no but i mean if he didn't look i'm not  a i'm not a judge jury i  i know a little bit about this case from  articles and online but i haven't seen  both facts laid out in their arguments  together but look if he if he did it  he's got 11 billion dollars  i wouldn't be showing up in court 511  billion dollars um but yeah look his  name got dragged through the mud right  and i hope that this is figured out i  think it's interesting that he basically  stole five percent of all eath  outstanding at the time ethereum was  around twenty dollars obviously now it  sits around four thousand dollars so  it's a very big increase in price  um but yeah look he moved it around it  seems to be a lot of evidence point to  that he did it obviously he's saying he  did not do it um and i guess the courts  will decide if this ever goes that  direction  so  i think that a huge piece of this is um  the public opinion right which we talked  about  two though is a lot of people  that now are showing up to the  cryptocurrency industry uh they don't  know about this hard fork that actually  created ethereum classic and ethereum  it's one of the reasons why a lot of  bitcoiners look at the ethereum um  uh  ecosystem and they have questions right  or or they're less bullish on it uh than  maybe they were before and the whole  idea was uh this was some uh kind of uh  centralized decision making that  occurred here to actually go ahead  create the hard fork uh uh to to  essentially try to roll back the chain  so that you could mitigate this theft  and so the thought process is like are  they just gonna play favorites over when  to do this and when not right if all of  a sudden john stole one eath from me uh  why don't they roll back the chain or  why don't they go ahead and and create a  hard fork so i get my one eat back right  oh is it because it's not enough money  is it because the community was involved  like there's 